course: dictionary
lesson: 4735
title: Words from the sea
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. Look at the words around it first. «Inside the Whale the Mariner stumped and he jumped and he thumped and he bumped, and danced hornpipes, giving the Whale hiccoughs.» Danced tells you a hornpipe is something you can dance, so here it means a dance.
explain: Some words come from a special world, like sailors and the sea. A hornpipe is a lively dance, often danced by one sailor. It can also mean the music for that dance, or an old wind instrument made with a horn. Clues such as dance, music, tune or instrument help you pick the right meaning.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Does «a lively sailors' dance» fit the Mariner's jumping and bumping? Yes, so the meaning works. Hiccoughs is an old spelling of hiccups, so the author is being playful with old words.
fact: A hornpipe is a lively dance, often danced by one sailor.
fact: The word can also mean the music for the dance, or an old wind instrument made with a horn.
fact: «The Sailor's Hornpipe» is a famous tune.
fact: «Hiccoughs» is an old spelling of hiccups.
exercise: «Inside the Whale the Mariner ... danced hornpipes.» What is a hornpipe in this sentence? | *A lively dance | A kind of sweet | A small boat | A loud sneeze
exercise: Lucy hears «The Sailor's Hornpipe» on the radio. What kind of thing is it? | *A famous tune | A bag of sweets | A type of sea bird | A ship's rope
exercise: The hornpipe is connected with which world? | Farms and tractors | Deserts and camels | Space rockets | *Sailors and the sea
test: A hornpipe can also mean an old wind instrument. What kind of instrument is it? | A harp | *An old wind instrument made with a horn | A drum | A piano
test: Which clue in the sentence tells you a hornpipe is a dance? | Hiccoughs | *Danced | Stumped and jumped | Whale
exam: Ruth says a hornpipe is a kind of bird. How can she check her meaning? | She can say nothing and guess | She can skip the word and never come back to it | *She can swap her meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | She can ignore the words around it
